nodecode-canvas is a rewrite of skk2010's skk2010/hermes-board-plugin:

  https://github.com/skk2010/hermes-board-plugin
  commit feb66592881bf76198cd8d790fd1dae25e5057f6
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Konstantin Sukhov (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the board itself. A board is still one JSON document on
disk, and the agent still draws on it with the same seven tools
(board_list, board_get, board_new, board_delete, board_apply, board_render)
and the same op vocabulary it had — nodes, edges, free lines, groups, notes,
captions, a title, a grid, and the layered `layout` placement — reimplemented
in Common Lisp. The renderer is new: the original's bundled Python library
drove a headless Chrome, this cell writes the same drawing as a standalone
SVG, wrapped in one HTML page that opens in any browser.

What was left out, and why: the desktop pane and the edit-watch loop
(board_watch, the wake of a session from the pane) are Hermes desktop UI, and
two processes sharing one open editor is not something a cell can own. PNG
output is gone with the browser it shelled out to — the SVG and the HTML page
carry the same picture, and nothing has to be installed to see them. From the
op vocabulary, `bus` (shared-trunk connections), `graph` (ranks derived from
the edges) and the table/split-compartment node kinds are not rewritten;
`layout` is, and notes, captions, groups and lines are.
